{"type": "say", "text": "Thank you, Mr. Lewis. I'd like to add a brief recap of my technology findings as well, since Dr. Morgan's earlier response was cut off. To reiterate: personal phones cannot be used for school recordings — the project must use district tablets. Source files must be stored in the restricted OralHistory drive, not on personal devices or the public school website. Recordings must be deleted exactly 45 days after the final class presentation, so indefinite retention is not permissible. And the technology plan must include a secure-storage configuration record documenting the restricted-drive setup. I'd also note that there's an important intersection between my findings and Mr. Lewis's consent findings — since five interviewees' recordings may only be used in the school's internal archive, the restricted OralHistory drive will be the appropriate storage location for those recordings, while the 15 recordings with public publication permission could potentially be published on the school website after being properly stored. I'm happy to help think through the storage and publication logistics if the committee would find that useful."}
